---
/*
  /lab/capture — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  THE QUESTION, Michael 2026-08-24: *"I was thinking about the ✚ that appears in
  the bottom right. My instinct tells me I should use this to add anything — a
  task, a quote, writing. But I realize I can't, and when I want to add a quote
  I have to shut down that idea and go to the fragment browser."*

  He is describing a promise the glyph makes and the button does not keep. A
  bare ✚ pinned bottom-right is the universal-create affordance by convention;
  `CaptureDialog.astro` labels it *"Write something down"*, and nobody reads a
  label on a FAB. There are only two honest endings — narrow the glyph (a
  pencil) or widen the promise — and this bench is about the second.

  ⚠ NOTHING IS BLOCKING IT TECHNICALLY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A UI QUESTION AND
  NOT AN ARCHITECTURE ONE. Three pieces of the plumbing already ship:

    · `notes.astro`'s → chooser already files a jot to the Agenda (a real
      prefilled, model-parsed TaskSheet), a log entry, a piece, or an append —
      with the undo table and the write-destination-THEN-consume ordering that
      14 §10e settled.
    · `?person=X&new=quote` (fragments.astro:63) already does *arrive in the
      owning room with the sheet open and prefilled*.
    · `/admin/notes?new=1` already opens a room's box from outside it.

  So this is assembly. What is NOT settled is the shape, and the shape is worth
  a bench because every proposition below is defensible on paper and they cost
  different numbers of taps at 390px with one thumb.

  ⚠ THE TRAP THE BENCH EXISTS TO CATCH: PUTTING THE QUESTION BEFORE THE TYPING.
  The premise of the box ([14 §4](../../../docs/plans/archive/14-capture.md)) is
  *as few decisions as possible between the thought and the saving of it*. A ✚
  that opens "task? quote? note?" taxes every capture with a classification, and
  taxes the COMMON case to serve the rare one — and it asks at the worst moment,
  before you have written the thing and often before you know what it is. That
  is the middle ground 14 §9a deleted, in a new costume. `menu` and `tabs` below
  are that costume, built honestly so it can win if it deserves to.

  ⚠ AND THE REAL FORK IS NOT "HOW MANY TAPS" — IT IS **WHO COLLECTS THE FIELDS**.
  `after`, `menu` and `chips` all hand off: they hold the body and send you to
  the room that owns the noun, whose sheet already knows about due dates,
  attribution and recurrence. `tabs` collects here, in the ✚, and the corner box
  slowly becomes four forms. Hold that while you look — it is the decision that
  cannot be reversed cheaply.

  ⚠ THE SAMPLE JOT DECIDES THE WINNER, so it is a control rather than a
  constant. A line that is obviously an errand flatters the variant that guesses;
  a line that is obviously a quote flatters the one that asks first; a line that
  is a half-thought flatters the one that asks nothing. Cycle all four before
  concluding anything, and use *(empty)* to type your own — the from-scratch
  motion is the one the ✚ is actually for.

  ── WHAT IS REAL HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT ──────────────────────────────────────

  14 §11a's lesson, learned the expensive way on the task parser: *prototype the
  query, not just the pixels* — a bench fed hand-written answers shows a parser
  that is always right, which is the thing in question. So:

    REAL · the editor. `mountRichEditor` from `src/scripts/rich-editor.ts`, the
           same one the shipped box mounts. Typing, the toolbar, paste and
           dictation behave here as they will there.
    REAL · the parse. Choosing the Agenda calls `actions.tasks.parse` for real
           and prints what came back and how long it took. It measured 1.5–4s
           when it shipped; a chooser that feels instant and then stalls four
           seconds is a different design from one that does not.
    REAL · the cost. Taps and elapsed time are COUNTED, not claimed. Every
           variant's badge is what you just did, not what I estimated. Same
           argument as 38's ratios: a quoted number goes stale, a measured one
           cannot.
    REAL · the jot, but only when you ask. "Write for real" arms the 700ms
           autosave against `fragments.saveWriting` with `status: note` — the
           actual shipped write path. Each panel keeps ONE id per sample, so
           re-typing updates a row rather than breeding them. Worst case is
           four notes in the pile, which is four taps to delete and is what the
           pile is for.
    NOT  · the filing. Choosing a destination prints the row it would write and
           links to the room it would drop you in. It does not write it — that
           IS the feature, and building it is what this bench decides.
    NOT  · images. `EditorToolbar` is mounted without `image`, so no upload path
           and no Supabase browser client. The bench is about the motion; the
           picture-into-a-jot path is already settled and unaffected.

  ⚠ THE FIFTH PROPOSITION IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. "It watches what you type and
  offers a destination once it sees a date" was proposed and Michael said leave
  it off (2026-08-24). Recorded rather than dropped: the recorded ban
  ([10-hq §4.21](../../../docs/plans/archive/10-hq.md)) is on a SILENT router,
  and an offer you confirm is arguably not that — but it is a second question,
  and putting it on the bench would have let a good suggestion engine rescue a
  bad chooser.

  ⚠ ONE SOURCE, FOUR RENDERS — the /lab/chrome rule. The shell below is one
  Astro fragment parameterised by `v.key`; the differences are what the
  proposition IS, not a second copy of the box. "Walk it" then MOVES that same
  DOM into a real `<dialog>` rather than rendering a second one, so the
  full-motion run and the side-by-side card can never drift. (ProseMirror
  survives the move: the editor element is the same node either side of it.)
*/
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import EditorToolbar from '../../components/admin/EditorToolbar.astro';
import EntityCombo from '../../components/admin/EntityCombo.astro';
import LinkDialog from '../../components/admin/LinkDialog.astro';
// ⚠ THE REAL SIX, IMPORTED RATHER THAN RETYPED. An earlier draft of this bench
// invented a `<select>` holding note/call/meal/gift — and `meal` is not a kind
// this app has. A bench that quietly ships a fifth vocabulary is worse than no
// bench: it makes the wrong thing feel considered.
import { KINDS } from '../../lib/hq/interactions';
// The Observatory's primitives, exactly as an admin page gets them: `hq.css`
// from AdminLayout, `admin.css` inlined by `app.css` (which `Base` brings).
import '../../styles/hq.css';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

/*
  ⚠ THIS QUERY IS THE OBSTACLE, NOT A CONVENIENCE. The person field has to be a
  real search-select over real people, and a search-select needs its options —
  so this page now does what the ✚ would have to do on EVERY admin page, since
  `AdminLayout` mounts the dialog on all of them and today it needs no server
  data at all. Feel the field, then remember this line is the price of it.

  ⚠ AND AN EMPTY ROSTER REMOVES THE TAB, which is 10-hq §10b's rule, not a bug:
  a picker with nothing in it is a control that asks a question with no answers.
  Signed out, RLS returns nothing and Log disappears from both tab rows — the
  header says so, so it does not read as breakage.
*/
const roster =
  (await Astro.locals.supabase.from('people').select('id, display_name').is('archived_at', null).order('display_name'))
    .data ?? [];
const people = roster.map((p) => ({ id: p.id, name: p.display_name }));

/**
 * The samples. Single-paragraph plain text on purpose — `setContent` is fed
 * these verbatim, and a bench is not the place to also be testing Markdown
 * round-tripping.
 *
 * Each one is a different SHAPE of thought, and that is the whole point of the
 * control: they do not all want the same door.
 */
const JOTS = [
  { key: 'errand', label: 'An errand', text: 'Call the dentist Friday and ask about the night guard' },
  {
    key: 'quote',
    label: 'A quote',
    text: '“The past is never dead. It’s not even past.” — William Faulkner, Requiem for a Nun',
  },
  { key: 'person', label: 'About a person', text: 'Coffee with Sam — he’s leaving the lab in October, wants to talk' },
  {
    key: 'seed',
    label: 'A half-thought',
    text: 'something about how the sky lab taught me that the thing you build to decide with is not the thing you ship',
  },
  { key: 'empty', label: '(empty)', text: '' },
] as const;

/**
 * The destinations.
 *
 * `arrival: true` is the short list — thoughts that ARRIVE and want somewhere
 * to go. The rest are curation-shaped: you do not dump a constellation, you sit
 * down and build one, and their rooms already carry a New door that
 * `PageHeader.astro`'s naming rule governs. The Everything/Arrival toggle is
 * here so the weight of the long list can be FELT rather than argued — six
 * rows is a chooser, twelve is a nav menu, and 14 §10a already called six grey
 * glyphs under one card a smell.
 */
const NOUNS = [
  {
    key: 'jot',
    label: 'Write it down',
    short: 'Jot',
    icon: 'ph:note',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'fragments · kind writing, status note',
    where: '/admin/notes',
    whereLabel: 'the pile',
  },
  {
    key: 'agenda',
    label: 'Add to the Agenda…',
    short: 'Task',
    icon: 'ph:calendar-blank',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'tasks — or events; the reading decides and the sheet says which',
    where: '/admin/agenda/tasks',
    whereLabel: 'Tasks',
  },
  {
    key: 'quote',
    label: 'Make it a quote…',
    short: 'Quote',
    icon: 'ph:quotes',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'fragments · kind quote — NOT a status flip: a different kind, and it wants attribution',
    where: '/admin/fragments',
    whereLabel: 'the Fragment Manager',
  },
  {
    key: 'piece',
    label: 'Make a piece',
    short: 'Piece',
    icon: 'ph:pen-nib',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'fragments · status note → draft (one flip, reversible)',
    where: '/admin/fragments',
    whereLabel: 'the writing sheet',
  },
  {
    key: 'append',
    label: 'Add to a piece…',
    short: 'Append',
    icon: 'ph:article',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'appended into another body — ⚠ the one motion with no undo (14 §10e)',
    where: '/admin/fragments',
    whereLabel: 'the piece it joined',
  },
  {
    key: 'log',
    label: 'Log an entry…',
    short: 'Log',
    icon: 'ph:note-pencil',
    arrival: true,
    row: 'interactions — needs a person, so the row is absent on an empty roster',
    where: '/admin/people',
    whereLabel: 'their profile',
  },
  {
    key: 'person',
    label: 'Add a person',
    short: 'Person',
    icon: 'ph:users-three',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'people',
    where: '/admin/people',
    whereLabel: 'the roster',
  },
  {
    key: 'goal',
    label: 'New goal',
    short: 'Goal',
    icon: 'ph:target',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'goals',
    where: '/admin/agenda/goals',
    whereLabel: 'Goals',
  },
  {
    key: 'song',
    label: 'New song',
    short: 'Song',
    icon: 'ph:music-notes',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'fragments · kind song',
    where: '/admin/sets',
    whereLabel: 'Listening',
  },
  {
    key: 'set',
    label: 'New set',
    short: 'Set',
    icon: 'ph:playlist',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'sets',
    where: '/admin/sets',
    whereLabel: 'Sets',
  },
  {
    key: 'constellation',
    label: 'New constellation',
    short: 'Suite',
    icon: 'ph:star',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'constellations',
    where: '/admin/constellations',
    whereLabel: 'the Sky',
  },
  {
    key: 'vocab',
    label: 'New term',
    short: 'Term',
    icon: 'ph:book-open',
    arrival: false,
    row: 'vocabulary',
    where: '/admin/library',
    whereLabel: 'the Library',
  },
] as const;

/**
 * The NARROWED tabs (`lite`), added 2026-08-24 after the full-tabs column was
 * picked and then costed against the real schemas and the real phone.
 *
 * Three things separate it from `tabs`, and each one is a named obstacle:
 *
 *  1. ⚠ THE TAB DECLARES; IT DOES NOT COLLECT. The box goes on autosaving a
 *     NOTE no matter which tab is lit — because a half-typed task cannot be
 *     saved as a task (`tasks.save` wants a title, `interactions.save` wants a
 *     person uuid), and the ✚'s whole promise is that the thought survives the
 *     typing of it. So the tab only decides where **Done** sends it, and the
 *     shipped note→X plumbing does the rest, ordering and undo included.
 *
 *  2. ⚠ AT MOST ONE FIELD, AND ONLY WHERE IT SAVES THE TRIP. Measured on the
 *     `tabs` column at 390px: its Task tab costs 319px of chrome, and with a
 *     keyboard up on a 390×844 phone that leaves ~157px for your words — on a
 *     375×667 phone, ~16px. One field folds that back to something a thumb can
 *     use. The field is `When` on the Agenda, `Said by` on a quote, `Who` on a
 *     log, and nothing at all on the other two.
 *
 *  3. ⚠ IT SAYS "AGENDA", NOT "TASK". 14 §10a settled that as ONE row for a
 *     reason Michael gave in his own words — *"I don't want the cognitive
 *     expenditure of thinking: is this more of an event or more of a task?"*
 *     A tab labelled Task reinstates exactly that, and moves it BEFORE the
 *     typing. `tabs` keeps the word so the two can be read side by side.
 *
 * `there` is what the destination room still has to ask for. It is on screen
 * because the only question that matters here is *did this trip get shorter*,
 * and a bench that shows what you collected without showing what you did not
 * would answer it dishonestly.
 */
const LITE = [
  { key: 'jot', label: 'Jot', field: null, kinds: false, becomes: '', there: '' },
  {
    key: 'agenda',
    label: 'Agenda',
    field: { label: 'When', type: 'date', placeholder: '' },
    kinds: false,
    becomes: 'Your words become — title: first line · notes: the rest',
    there: 'the time, effort, lead, repeat and goal — and whether this is a task or an event',
  },
  {
    key: 'quote',
    label: 'Quote',
    field: { label: 'Said by', type: 'text', placeholder: 'Who said it' },
    kinds: false,
    becomes: 'Your words become the quote itself',
    there: 'from what, where in it, the source link, shared by, subjects',
  },
  {
    key: 'piece',
    label: 'Piece',
    field: null,
    kinds: false,
    becomes: 'Your words become the draft — nothing to collect',
    there: 'the title, and the writing',
  },
  {
    key: 'log',
    label: 'Log',
    /*
      ⚠ `combo`, NOT A TEXT INPUT — and the difference is the whole reason this
      tab is the one that tests the design. `interactions.save` wants
      `personId: z.uuid()`; a typed name is not a person. So the control has to
      CONSTRAIN, which is exactly what `EntityCombo` was built to do for the
      quote sheet's provenance facets: pick an existing row and commit its id,
      or explicitly choose to create one.

      ⚠ ITS "＋ Add «name»" ROW IS A REAL OPEN QUESTION, left visible on purpose.
      Logging about somebody not yet on the roster — "coffee with Sam" where Sam
      is new — is an ordinary capture, and the log action cannot do it: it would
      need a `people` row written first. Either the ✚ owns that two-step or the
      combo has to refuse names it does not know. The receipt says which of the
      two happened, so the case can be met rather than discovered later.
    */
    field: { label: 'Who', type: 'combo', placeholder: 'Who was it?' },
    kinds: true,
    becomes: 'Your words become the entry’s note',
    there: 'the others who were there, and the date',
  },
] as const;

/**
 * ⚠ THE LOG TAB IS CONDITIONAL, in both tab rows (10-hq §10b, the same rule
 * `notes.astro` applies to its chooser row and `TaskSheet` to its goal picker).
 */
const LITE_TABS = LITE.filter((t) => t.key !== 'log' || people.length > 0);

/** The chooser never offers "write it down" as a destination — you are already there. */
const DESTINATIONS = NOUNS.filter((n) => n.key !== 'jot');
/** `tabs` and `chips` need a few short labels, not twelve. These four are the arrival-shaped ones that collect fields. */
const FEW = (['agenda', 'quote', 'piece', 'log'] as const).filter((k) => k !== 'log' || people.length > 0);

/**
 * The four propositions. `claim` is what each one asserts; `cost` is what it
 * asks of you BEFORE you have typed anything, which is the axis 14 §4 cares
 * about most.
 */
const VARIANTS = [
  {
    key: 'after',
    name: 'Box first, file after',
    claim:
      'Today’s box, plus the pile’s own → chooser in the footer. Type first, classify after — usually you only know what it is once it is written.',
    cost: 'nothing before typing · two taps to file',
  },
  {
    key: 'menu',
    name: 'Menu first',
    claim:
      'The ✚ opens the list of nouns, “Write it down” on top. Honest about the glyph. Asks the question at the moment you may not be able to answer it.',
    cost: 'one decision before typing · one tap to file',
  },
  {
    key: 'chips',
    name: 'Chips in the footer',
    claim:
      'Destinations always visible under the box. One tap instead of two — at the price of permanent chrome under a box whose design says it needs no introduction.',
    cost: 'nothing before typing · one tap to file',
  },
  {
    key: 'lite',
    name: 'Kind tabs, narrowed',
    claim:
      'The tab DECLARES where this is going; the box keeps writing notes underneath. One field per kind at most, folded on a phone, and Done never blocks — the room collects the rest.',
    cost: 'one decision before typing · one field, at most',
  },
  {
    key: 'tabs',
    name: 'Kind tabs in the box',
    claim:
      'The ✚ becomes a small composer: pick a kind and the box grows the fields that kind needs. Fastest when you already know — and the only one that collects HERE rather than handing off.',
    cost: 'one decision before typing · fields collected in the corner',
  },
] as const;
---

<Base title="Capture — bench">
  <div class="lab" data-view="grid" data-w="wide" data-nouns="arrival" data-write="off">
    <header class="lab__head">
      <h1>What the ✚ is for</h1>
      <p>
        Five propositions for one button, on one jot. The editor is the real one and the Agenda parse is a real model
        call — see the file header for what is simulated and why. Judge <strong
          >how many decisions come before the typing</strong
        >, and <strong>who collects the fields</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="lab__ctl">
        <button type="button" data-view="grid" aria-pressed="true">Side by side</button>
        <button type="button" data-view="solo" aria-pressed="false">One at a time</button>
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" data-w="wide" aria-pressed="true">Desk</button>
        <button type="button" data-w="phone" aria-pressed="false">390px</button>
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" data-nouns="arrival" aria-pressed="true">Arrival-shaped (6)</button>
        <button type="button" data-nouns="all" aria-pressed="false">Everything (12)</button>
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" data-theme-flip>Flip theme</button>
      </div>

      <div class="lab__ctl lab__ctl--jots">
        <span class="lab__ctlk">Jot</span>
        {
          JOTS.map((j, i) => (
            <button type="button" data-jot={j.key} data-text={j.text} aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}>
              {j.label}
            </button>
          ))
        }
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {
          /* ⚠ OFF BY DEFAULT AND SAYING SO. The switch arms the real shipped
            write path against the real pile — it is the only thing on this page
            that touches the database, and a bench that quietly wrote rows would
            be the kind of surprise the ground rules exist to prevent. */
        }
        <span class="lab__ctlk">Jot writes</span>
        <button type="button" data-write="off" aria-pressed="true">Dry run</button>
        <button type="button" data-write="on" aria-pressed="false">For real → the pile</button>
      </div>

      {
        people.length === 0 && (
          <p class="lab__q">
            <b>No roster on this session, so the Log tab is absent from both tab rows.</b> That is 10-hq §10b's rule
            rather than a fault — a picker with nothing in it asks a question with no answers. Sign in to the admin in
            this browser and reload to see the person field for real.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <p class="lab__q">
        <b>The question to hold while you look:</b> the ✚ exists so a thought survives the ten seconds between having it and
        saving it. Every tap you add is spent from that budget — but so is every trip to another room afterwards, and
        <em>that</em> is the cost you are currently paying for a quote. Which of these spends the budget in the place you
        would rather spend it?
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="lab__grid">
      {
        VARIANTS.map((v) => (
          <section class="prop" data-prop={v.key}>
            <header class="prop__head">
              <div class="prop__title">
                <span class="prop__fab" aria-hidden="true">
                  <Icon name="ph:plus" />
                </span>
                <h2>{v.name}</h2>
              </div>
              <p class="prop__claim">{v.claim}</p>
              <p class="prop__cost">{v.cost}</p>
            </header>

            {/* ── ONE SOURCE. This frame is what "Walk it" moves into the dialog. ── */}
            <div class="prop__frame" data-frame={v.key}>
              <div class="cap-shell font-sans">
                {/* `menu` only: the dialog opens on the list, and choosing swaps
                    it for the box. Deliberately INSIDE the dialog rather than a
                    popover at the corner — a corner popover is the cheaper reach
                    for a thumb and is the sub-variant this bench does not build;
                    it keeps the ✚'s job single ("open the thing") at the price of
                    a full dialog you may not have wanted yet. */}
                {v.key === 'menu' && (
                  <div class="lab-menu" data-menu>
                    <p class="lab-menu__k">What is it?</p>
                    <div class="lab-menu__list">
                      {NOUNS.map((n) => (
                        <button
                          type="button"
                          class="pop__row"
                          data-pick={n.key}
                          data-label={n.label}
                          data-row={n.row}
                          data-where={n.where}
                          data-where-label={n.whereLabel}
                          data-arrival={String(n.arrival)}
                          data-tap
                        >
                          <Icon name={n.icon} /> {n.label}
                        </button>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                )}

                <div class="lab-body" data-body>
                  {/* `tabs` only: the box grows a kind, and the kind grows fields. */}
                  {v.key === 'tabs' && (
                    <div class="lab-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="What kind of thing">
                      <button type="button" class="lab-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-tab="jot" data-tap>
                        Jot
                      </button>
                      {FEW.map((k) => {
                        const n = NOUNS.find((x) => x.key === k)!;
                        return (
                          <button type="button" class="lab-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab={k} data-tap>
                            {n.short}
                          </button>
                        );
                      })}
                    </div>
                  )}

                  {/* `lite` only: the same row, saying Agenda where the full one
                      says Task — see the LITE note in the frontmatter. */}
                  {v.key === 'lite' && (
                    <div class="lab-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Where this is going">
                      {LITE_TABS.map((t, i) => (
                        <button
                          type="button"
                          class="lab-tab"
                          role="tab"
                          aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}
                          data-tab={t.key}
                          data-field={t.field?.label}
                          data-becomes={t.becomes}
                          data-there={t.there}
                          data-tap
                        >
                          {t.label}
                        </button>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                  )}

                  <div class="cap-box" data-box />

                  <EditorToolbar class="cap-tools" />

                  {/* `tabs` only: the fields the chosen kind needs, in the ✚.

                      ⚠ BELOW THE TOOLBAR, NOT ABOVE IT — and the first draft of
                      this bench had it the other way, which looked wrong the
                      moment it was on screen. `.cap-tools` belongs to the box:
                      it formats the words directly above it, and a pair of text
                      inputs wedged between them breaks that adjacency. The rule
                      `CaptureDialog` already states for the toolbar ("under the
                      box, never over it") is really a rule about the toolbar
                      TOUCHING the box.

                      ⚠ THIS IS THE PROPOSITION'S WHOLE CLAIM AND ITS WHOLE RISK.
                      Two fields look harmless; the shipped TaskSheet has date,
                      time, effort, lead, recurrence and a goal picker, and the
                      quote sheet has attribution, source and a subject field.
                      Whatever is not here is a second trip anyway — so read
                      these as the FLOOR, not the finished form. */}
                  {v.key === 'tabs' && (
                    <div class="lab-fields" data-fields hidden>
                      {/* ⚠ EVERY SET SAYS WHAT BECOMES OF YOUR WORDS, because
                          the question "what happens to what I already typed"
                          is the first one this proposition provokes and the
                          box was answering it silently. The split for a task
                          is not invented here — it is what 14 §10c already
                          settled for a dump filed from the pile: the first
                          line is the title, the rest is the notes, because a
                          task title is a row you scan on a Tuesday morning. */}
                      <div class="lab-fields__set" data-for="agenda" hidden>
                        <p class="lab-becomes">
                          Your words become — <b>title:</b> first line · <b>notes:</b> the rest
                        </p>
                        <label class="f">
                          <span class="f__k">Date</span>
                          <input type="date" class="f--narrow" />
                        </label>
                        <label class="f">
                          <span class="f__k">Time</span>
                          <input type="time" class="f--narrow" />
                        </label>
                        <button type="button" class="lab-mini" data-parse data-tap>
                          Read the sentence
                        </button>
                      </div>
                      <div class="lab-fields__set" data-for="quote" hidden>
                        <p class="lab-becomes">
                          Your words become <b>the quote itself</b>
                        </p>
                        <label class="f" data-hold="Said by">
                          <span class="f__k">
                            Said by <em>required</em>
                          </span>
                          <input type="text" placeholder="Who said it" />
                        </label>
                        <label class="f" data-hold="Where in it">
                          <span class="f__k">Where in it</span>
                          <input type="text" placeholder="Source" />
                        </label>
                      </div>
                      {people.length > 0 && (
                        <div class="lab-fields__set" data-for="log" hidden>
                          <p class="lab-becomes">
                            Your words become <b>the entry’s note</b>
                          </p>
                          <label class="f" data-hold="Who">
                            <span class="f__k">
                              Who <em>required</em>
                            </span>
                            <EntityCombo
                              id="combo-tabs-log"
                              name="name-tabs-log"
                              idName="id-tabs-log"
                              noun="person"
                              options={people}
                              placeholder="Who was it?"
                            />
                          </label>
                          {/* ⚠ WAS A `<select>` HOLDING FOUR INVENTED VALUES, one of
                            which (`meal`) is not a kind this app has. Six chips,
                            one tap, the real `KINDS` with the marks 12 §6 chose
                            to keep distinct — a menu you have to open to see is
                            the wrong shape for six options that fit on screen. */}
                          <div class="lab-kinds" data-hold="Kind" data-hold-default role="group" aria-label="Kind">
                            {KINDS.map((k, i) => (
                              <button
                                type="button"
                                class="lab-kind"
                                data-kind={k.key}
                                data-kind-label={k.label}
                                aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}
                                data-tap
                              >
                                <Icon name={k.icon} /> {k.label}
                              </button>
                            ))}
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      )}
                      <div class="lab-fields__set" data-for="piece" hidden>
                        <p class="lab-becomes">
                          Your words become <b>the draft</b> — nothing to collect. One flip, reversible.
                        </p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  )}

                  {/* `lite` only: the one control, and it is NOT here at rest.

                      ⚠ MEASURED, THEN REDRAWN. The first cut put a folded chip
                      inside its own bordered section and it saved almost
                      nothing: 258px of chrome against the full column's 319px,
                      because a section with padding, a gap and a border costs
                      ~96px to hold one chip. The tab row is cheap (36px); the
                      SECTION was the expense.

                      So at rest this does not exist. The kind's one control is a
                      chip in the foot — a row that is already there and already
                      paid for — and this section unfolds only once you ask for
                      it. Resting chrome is then 162px on every tab, which is the
                      36px of tabs and nothing else.

                      ⚠ AND THE EXPLANATION MOVED OUT OF THE BOX ENTIRELY. "What
                      your words become" and "what the room still asks for" now
                      sit under the card, because bench annotation inside the
                      frame is chrome the product would never ship — and it was
                      being counted against the very budget this column exists to
                      protect. A bench that inflates what it is measuring is
                      measuring itself. */}
                  {v.key === 'lite' && (
                    <div class="lab-fields lab-fields--lite" data-fields hidden>
                      {LITE_TABS.filter((t) => t.field).map((t) => (
                        <div class="lab-fields__set" data-for={t.key} hidden>
                          <label class="f" data-hold={t.field!.label}>
                            <span class="f__k">
                              {t.field!.label}
                              {t.key === 'log' && <em>required</em>}
                            </span>
                            {t.field!.type === 'combo' ? (
                              <EntityCombo
                                id={`combo-lite-${t.key}`}
                                name={`name-lite-${t.key}`}
                                idName={`id-lite-${t.key}`}
                                noun="person"
                                options={people}
                                placeholder={t.field!.placeholder}
                              />
                            ) : (
                              <input
                                type={t.field!.type}
                                placeholder={t.field!.placeholder}
                                class={t.field!.type === 'date' ? 'f--narrow' : ''}
                              />
                            )}
                          </label>

                          {/* ⚠ THE KIND RIDES ALONG, AND IT COSTS NOTHING AT REST.
                              "One field per kind" is about what you must ANSWER,
                              not about how many controls exist once you have
                              opened the disclosure — and LogSheet already states
                              the split this depends on: *"the kind and the date
                              both have honest defaults; there is no defensible
                              default for whose life this was."* So Who is the
                              field, and the kind sits beside it already answered,
                              changeable in one tap. */}
                          {t.kinds && (
                            <div class="lab-kinds" data-hold="Kind" data-hold-default role="group" aria-label="Kind">
                              {KINDS.map((k, i) => (
                                <button
                                  type="button"
                                  class="lab-kind"
                                  data-kind={k.key}
                                  data-kind-label={k.label}
                                  aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}
                                  data-tap
                                >
                                  <Icon name={k.icon} /> {k.label}
                                </button>
                              ))}
                            </div>
                          )}
                        </div>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                  )}

                  <div class="cap-foot">
                    <span class="cap-status" data-status aria-live="polite" />

                    {/* `chips` only: the destinations, always on screen. */}
                    {v.key === 'chips' && (
                      <div class="lab-chips" data-chips>
                        {DESTINATIONS.map((n) => (
                          <button
                            type="button"
                            class="lab-chip"
                            data-pick={n.key}
                            data-label={n.label}
                            data-row={n.row}
                            data-where={n.where}
                            data-where-label={n.whereLabel}
                            data-arrival={String(n.arrival)}
                            data-tap
                          >
                            {n.short}
                          </button>
                        ))}
                      </div>
                    )}

                    {v.key !== 'chips' && <span class="lab-spacer" />}

                    {/* The kind's one control, in a row that already exists. */}
                    {v.key === 'lite' && (
                      <button type="button" class="lab-fold" data-fold hidden data-tap>
                        <Icon name="ph:plus" /> <span data-fold-label />
                      </button>
                    )}

                    {/* `after` only: the pile's own control, moved to the box. */}
                    {v.key === 'after' && (
                      <button
                        type="button"
                        class="btn btn-sm font-sans"
                        popovertarget={`pop-${v.key}`}
                        data-chooser
                        data-tap
                      >
                        <Icon name="ph:arrow-right" /> Turn it into…
                      </button>
                    )}

                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm font-sans" data-newjot data-tap>
                      <Icon name="ph:plus" /> New
                    </button>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm font-sans" data-done data-tap>
                      Done
                    </button>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              {/* ⚠ INSIDE THE FRAME, AND THAT IS TRAP 7 ARRIVING ON SCHEDULE.
                This chooser was mounted at page level first — the way
                `notes.astro` mounts its one menu for the whole pile — and it
                worked on the card and was DEAD in the walk-through: `showModal`
                makes everything outside the dialog inert, so a popover sitting
                out there cannot be shown from a button sitting in here. It
                failed silently, which is the signature of the whole trap 7
                family: no error, no console, a control that simply does
                nothing.

                Nesting is safe for a popover in a way it is not for a
                `<dialog>` (CaptureDialog's own note): the top layer takes it
                above its modal opener, and there is no second focus trap to
                fight. And because it lives in the frame, it MOVES with the
                frame — one chooser, both contexts, nothing to keep in step. */}
              {v.key === 'after' && (
                <div class="pop pop--wide" popover id={`pop-${v.key}`}>
                  {DESTINATIONS.map((n) => (
                    <button
                      type="button"
                      class="pop__row"
                      data-pick={n.key}
                      data-label={n.label}
                      data-row={n.row}
                      data-where={n.where}
                      data-where-label={n.whereLabel}
                      data-arrival={String(n.arrival)}
                      data-tap
                    >
                      <Icon name={n.icon} /> {n.label}
                    </button>
                  ))}
                </div>
              )}
            </div>

            {/* Bench annotation, deliberately OUTSIDE the frame — see the note
              on the fields section above. */}
            <p class="prop__note" data-note hidden />

            {/* The card keeps its place in the grid while the frame is on stage. */}
            <p class="prop__onstage" data-onstage hidden>
              …in the dialog. Close it to bring the box back here.
            </p>

            <div class="prop__foot">
              <button type="button" class="lab-mini" data-walk={v.key}>
                Walk it from the corner ↗
              </button>
              <span class="prop__cost prop__cost--live" data-meter>
                not touched yet
              </span>
            </div>

            <output class="prop__receipt" data-receipt hidden />
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The corner ✚ — the shipped `.cap-fab`, appearing only during a walk. */}
  <button id="lab-fab" class="cap-fab" type="button" aria-label="Write something down" hidden>
    <Icon name="ph:plus" class="text-2xl" />
  </button>

  <dialog id="lab-stage" class="modal-dialog w-full max-w-xl" aria-label="Write something down"></dialog>

  {
    /* ⚠ ONE PER EDITOR, NOT ONE SHARED. `mountRichEditor` wires its link
      command to the dialog it is handed, so a single dialog behind four
      editors would apply the link you just typed to all four of them — a bench
      artefact that would read as a bug in the box. */
  }
  {VARIANTS.map((v) => <LinkDialog id={`lab-link-${v.key}`} />)}
</Base>

